# Scenario-based, condensed. Book A: Metasploit/Meterpreter. Covers Ch.1-7.
DECKS=[("SA","Metasploit & Meterpreter - Scenarios (Ch.1-7)",[
("mc","A scanner reports a server has CVE-2024-1234 with a CVSS of 9.8, and a second server with no CVEs but admins log in over plain telnet with a shared password. Which do you worry about, and why?",
 ["Both - the misconfigured telnet box is at least as dangerous, and attackers often prefer misconfigs since they need no exploit and raise no alert","Only the CVE box - a high CVSS always wins","Only the telnet box - CVEs are never exploited","Neither - no CVE means no risk"],0,
 "Ch.1: misconfigurations are at least as important as CVEs and are often preferred. CVE = the bug's name, CVSS = its 0-10 score."),
("mc","You have valid administrator credentials for a fully patched, properly configured Windows box. Can Metasploit still get you in?",
 ["Yes - known admin creds let Metasploit give you a Meterpreter shell even on a hardened box","No - patching blocks all Metasploit access","Only if there's an unpatched CVE","Only over the physical console"],0,
 "Ch.1: credentials beat configuration. This is the whole setup for the book."),
("mc","You land a Meterpreter session but 'getuid' shows a low-privileged user. What's true about what you can do and where you can go?",
 ["What you can see depends on your process's privileges, and you can migrate from a higher-privileged process down to lower ones","You automatically have SYSTEM","You can migrate from low privilege up to SYSTEM for free","Meterpreter can't do recon until you're admin"],0,
 "Ch.2: data available depends on the host process's privileges; migration goes high->low. Meterpreter lives in a process in memory, which is why file scanning misses it."),
("sa","Scenario: your lure is a tiny buffer overflow with almost no room for code, and later you phish a laptop on flaky hotel wifi that may drop after one connection. Which payload type for each, and why?",
 "Tiny space -> STAGED (small stub downloads the rest). Flaky one-shot network -> STAGELESS (whole payload ships at once, needs no second connection).","Ch.3 exam favorite. Name tell: slash = staged (meterpreter/reverse_tcp), underscore = stageless (meterpreter_reverse_tcp)."),
("mc","Your payload needs to call home through a corporate firewall that blocks inbound connections. Bind or reverse?",
 ["Reverse - the target connects out to you (LHOST/LPORT), and outbound is usually allowed","Bind - the target listens and you connect in","Either works equally","Neither works through a firewall"],0,
 "Ch.3: reverse beats firewalls/NAT. A handler catches the callback; run it as a job (exploit -j) in the background."),
("sa","You need to brute force one service and have limited time. The book says which targets/services are faster, and what 4 things define the attack?",
 "Windows is faster than Linux, and SMB is faster than SSH (Windows Wins, SMB is Speedy). The 4 features: userlist, wordlist, time, defenses (Users Want Time Delays).","Ch.4. Modules: smb_login and ssh_login. Defenses = lockout, rate limiting, MFA, strong passwords."),
("mc","You phish a user and they type the WRONG password into your fake Windows prompt. What happens with phish_windows_credentials?",
 ["It keeps re-prompting until the credentials actually validate against the system","It accepts anything and logs the wrong password","It crashes","It locks the account"],0,
 "Ch.5: that re-prompt is why it works. You can run it from an UNPRIVILEGED session to steal the logged-on user's domain/local creds."),
("mc","You generated a payload and ran it through Metasploit encoders several times, but Defender still catches it. Why?",
 ["Encoders dodge bad characters, not AV - modern AV watches behavior, memory and the cloud","You didn't encode enough times","Encoding only works on Linux","The payload was corrupted"],0,
 "Ch.6/7: encoding != evasion. Whether malware runs depends on the target's AV; Windows ships Defender."),
("sa","You want to trojanize a real program so the victim sees normal behavior. Which tool and flags, and what does static vs dynamic analysis mean for the defender?",
 "msfvenom with -x <real program> -k (keep it working), plus -p payload, -f format, -o outfile. Static = examine without running (strings, hashes, Ghidra). Dynamic = run and watch (strace, gdb).","Ch.6."),
("mc","You're admin on a box with Defender tamper protection ON. You can't disable Defender - what's your move for a lasting blind spot?",
 ["Add an exclusion - excluded paths/processes are never scanned, and admins can still add them even with tamper protection on","Disable Defender in the registry (still supported)","Empty the quarantine","Nothing - tamper protection stops everything"],0,
 "Ch.7: this is the key nuance. Quarantined threats can also be restored; registry-disable is no longer supported."),
])]